    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required `riskFlag` to be `true` on the owner read response when **any** of three signals hold: possible duplicate, disposable-adjacent email domain, or city over its soft capacity \u2014 otherwise `false`.\n\nIn this codebase the `POST /api/owners` pipeline already persists two of these signals onto the `Owner` entity (`possibleDuplicate` via `FlagOwnerPossibleDuplicate`, `capacityWarning` \u2014 the \"soft capacity\" band of 40\u201349 \u2014 via `FlagOwnerCityCapacity`), and `riskFlag` is a read-time derived field, so I computed it in the mapper alongside the other derived fields.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `riskFlag` boolean (`readOnly`) property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field. Description mirrors the spec's three conditions.\n\n2. **`DisposableEmailDomains.java`** (new) \u2014 a small helper holding the disposable blocklist with `isBlocked(email)` and `isDisposableAdjacent(email)`. \"Disposable-adjacent\" means the domain shares a blocked domain's registrable base label \u2014 same second-level name under any TLD, or a subdomain of a blocked domain (e.g. `mailinator.net`, `x.mailinator.com` for the blocked `mailinator.com`). Exactly-blocked domains count as adjacent too; absent/malformed emails are not.\n\n3. **`ValidateOwnerEmailDomain.java`** \u2014 refactored to reuse `DisposableEmailDomains.isBlocked(...)` so the blocklist has a single source of truth. Behavior is unchanged (still rejects exact blocked domains with 400; leaves absent/blank untouched).\n\n4. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added `@Mapping(target = \"riskFlag\", ...)` and a `riskFlag(Owner)` method: `possibleDuplicate || disposableAdjacent || capacityWarning`, each null-safe via `Boolean.TRUE.equals(...)`.\n\nTests: `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The `coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` acceptance test (a fully-unique owner with no email, unique city, no duplicate) correctly returns `riskFlag: false`.\n\nOne design note worth surfacing: since a saved owner can never carry an *exactly*-blocked disposable domain (those are rejected with 400 at create time), the disposable-adjacent read-time signal only ever fires on the *related* variants (subdomains / alternate TLDs), which is why I implemented it as base-label matching rather than an exact-list lookup.",
